1. What is your Grocery App about?

My Grocery App is a full-stack web application that allows users to browse products, add them to a cart, place orders, and make secure payments using Stripe.
It also includes authentication, order tracking, and admin features for product and order management.

2. What was your motivation for building this project?

I wanted to build something practical that uses real-world features like authentication, database integration, and online payment systems.
The project helped me understand how e-commerce systems work from both frontend and backend perspectives.

3. What is unique about your app?

Unlike typical demo apps, mine integrates a real payment gateway (Stripe) and includes admin-side functionalities like viewing all orders and managing product data.
It’s also designed with a scalable backend and responsive UI optimized for both desktop and mobile users.

⚙️ B. Technical Implementation
🔹 1. Frontend (React.js)
4. Why did you choose React for the frontend?

React’s component-based structure makes it easy to build dynamic UIs, manage product states (like cart items), and handle real-time UI updates efficiently.
It also integrates easily with REST APIs.

5. How did you manage state (like cart items or login)?

I used Context API or Zustand to manage global state.
This allowed me to share the same data (like cart items or logged-in user) across different components without prop drilling.

6. How did you make the UI responsive?

I used TailwindCSS for quick and consistent styling. It allows using utility classes to make layouts adaptive for different screen sizes.

🔹 2. Backend (Node.js + Express)
7. Why did you choose Express.js for the backend?

Express is lightweight, fast, and easy to integrate with middleware for authentication, logging, and data validation.
It allowed me to build RESTful APIs quickly for handling users, products, and orders.

8. How is your backend structured?

I followed a modular MVC pattern:

models/ – MongoDB schemas (User, Product, Order)

controllers/ – business logic (add to cart, create order, etc.)

routes/ – endpoint definitions

middleware/ – auth validation, error handling

9. What middleware did you use?

express.json() – to parse JSON request bodies

cors() – to allow cross-origin communication

cookie-parser – to read JWT cookies

protectRoute – custom middleware for authentication verification

10. How did you handle authentication?

I used JWT (JSON Web Tokens) for authentication.
When the user logs in, a JWT token is generated and stored as an httpOnly cookie for security.
This token is verified on every protected route to confirm user identity.

11. What is bcrypt and why did you use it?

Bcrypt is used for hashing passwords before saving them in the database.
It adds salt to make hashes unique and resistant to brute-force attacks.

🔹 3. Database (MongoDB + Mongoose)
12. Why did you use MongoDB instead of SQL?

MongoDB’s flexible schema is perfect for unstructured or nested data like products, categories, and cart items.
It’s also highly scalable and integrates seamlessly with Node.js via Mongoose.

13. What models did you create?

User Model: name, email, password, cartItems, orders

Product Model: name, category, price, stock, image

Order Model: userId, items, totalAmount, paymentStatus

14. How do you relate users, products, and orders?

I used MongoDB references (ObjectId).
Each order stores a reference to the user who placed it and the products purchased.
Then I used Mongoose’s .populate() to fetch related data easily.

💳 C. Payment Integration (Stripe)
15. How did you integrate Stripe?

I used the official Stripe SDK to create payment intents and verify transactions using webhooks.
The backend listens to Stripe’s webhook events to confirm payment success or failure.

16. How do Stripe Webhooks work?

Webhooks are special endpoints that Stripe calls automatically after a payment event.
My webhook verifies the Stripe signature, checks if the payment succeeded, updates the order as “paid,” and clears the user’s cart.

17. How do you ensure payment security?

Use Stripe’s secret key (stored in .env)

Validate webhook signatures

Use HTTPS for frontend communication

Store only necessary payment info, not card details

🌐 D. Deployment & Integration
18. Where did you deploy the app?

Frontend: Vercel

Backend: Render or Railway

Database: MongoDB Atlas
All services are cloud-based, making the app accessible and scalable.

19. How do frontend and backend communicate?

The frontend communicates with the backend via Axios for API calls (like /api/order or /api/auth/login).
CORS is enabled in backend to allow cross-origin requests.

20. How did you handle CORS errors?

By enabling CORS middleware in backend:

app.use(cors({
  origin: "http://localhost:5173",
  credentials: true
}));

